 To find out how many baggies Maria can make, she needs to determine how many sets of 8 cookies she can assemble with the cookies she has. First, she'll make baggies using only chocolate chip cookies. The maximum number of baggies using chocolate chip cookies is when she uses all of them, so she would divide the number of chocolate chip cookies by the number of cookies per bag.

\begin{align*}
\text{Number of baggies with chocolate chip cookies} &= \frac{5 \text{ chocolate chip cookies}}{8 \text{ cookies per bag}}\\
&= \boxed{0.625} \text{ baggies}
\end{align*}

Since Maria cannot make a fraction of a baggie, she cannot make baggies using only the chocolate chip cookies.

Next, she will try making baggies with the oatmeal cookies. She has 19 oatmeal cookies, and she can still make as many baggies as possible, so she divides the number of oatmeal cookies by the number of cookies per bag:

\begin{align*}
\text{Number of baggies with oatmeal cookies} &= \frac{19 \text{ oatmeal cookies}}{8 \text{ cookies per bag}}\\
&= \boxed{2.375} \text{ baggies}
\end{align*}

Since Maria cannot make a fraction of a baggie, she cannot make baggies using only the oatmeal cookies either.

Maria needs to combine the remaining chocolate chip cookies with the oatmeal cookies to assemble a whole number of baggies. She can definitely use the remaining 4 oatmeal cookies (19 - 15 = 4) to make one baggie. As for the 5 chocolate chip cookies, they can be used to create multiple baggies if she uses them all with the remaining oatmeal cookies. The maximum number of baggies that can be made using the remaining oatmeal cookies and all the chocolate chip cookies is when she uses all the chocolate chip cookies, but that would mean she has used more oatmeal cookies than she has:

\begin{align*}
\text{Number of chocolate chip cookies} + \text{Number of oatmeal cookies left} &= 5 + 4\\
&= 9\\
\text{Number of cookies this combination can be divided by 8 (number of cookies per bag)} &\geq 9\\
\text{Number of baggies with the combination of cookies} &\geq \frac{9}{8}\\
&= \boxed{1.125} \text{ baggies}
\end{align*}

However, since Maria cannot make a fraction of a baggie, she can only make \boxed{1} baggie with the remaining oatmeal cookies and the chocolate chip cookies.

So, Maria can make \boxed{1} baggie of cookies as a whole.

