\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{amsmath}
\usepackage{empheq}
\usepackage{box}
\usepackage{mathtools}

\begin{document}

Can you solve the following math problem?  A waiter had 47 customers to wait on. If 41 customers left and he got another 20 customers, how many customers would he have?  Explain your reasoning. Your final answer should be a single numerical number, in the form \boxed{answer}, at the end of your response.

\begin{empheq}[box=\fbox]{align}
47 &\text{ original customers }\\
- 41 &\text{ customers that left }\\
+ 20 &\text{ new customers } \\
\boxed{answer} &= \boxed{9}
\end{empheq}

The final number of customers would be 9 customers.  You can verify this answer by checking that:

\begin{empheq}[box=\fbox]{align*}
&\text{ (Number of customers)} \\
&= 47 - 41 + 20 \\
&= \boxed{9} \\
\end{empheq}

\end{document}

